        "\n",
        "### **Question 3: Describe the purpose of activation function in neural networks. Compare Sigmoid, ReLU, and Tanh functions.**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Purpose of Activation Function:**\n",
        "Activation functions introduce **non-linearity** into the neural network, allowing it to learn complex relationships between inputs and outputs.\n",
        "Without them, a neural network — no matter how many layers — would behave like a **linear model**.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "#### **Common Activation Functions:**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Function                         | Formula                                                     | Range   | Pros                               | Cons                             |   |                 |\n",
        "| -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------- | - | --------------- |\n",
        "| **Sigmoid**                      | ( f(x) = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-x}} )                             | (0, 1)  | Smooth, probabilistic output       | Vanishing gradient for large     | x | , slow training |\n",
        "| **Tanh**                         | ( f(x) = \\tanh(x) = \\frac{e^{x} - e^{-x}}{e^{x} + e^{-x}} ) | (-1, 1) | Zero-centered, stronger gradient   | Still suffers vanishing gradient |   |                 |\n",
        "| **ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit)** | ( f(x) = \\max(0, x) )                                       | [0, ∞)  | Fast convergence, less computation | “Dead ReLU” for negative x       |   |                 |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "#### **Comparison Summary:**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Scenario                                     | Best Choice |\n",
        "| -------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n",
        "| Output between 0 and 1 (e.g., probabilities) | **Sigmoid** |\n",
        "| Hidden layers with normalized input          | **Tanh**    |\n",
        "| Deep networks, large datasets                | **ReLU**    |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### **Question 4: What is the difference between Loss function and Cost function in neural networks? Provide examples.**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Term              | Definition                                                    | Example                   |\n",
        "| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n",
        "| **Loss Function** | Measures the error for **a single training example**.         | Binary Cross-Entropy, MSE |\n",
        "| **Cost Function** | The **average of all losses** across the entire training set. | Mean Binary Cross-Entropy |\n",
        "\n",
        "**In Simple Terms:**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Loss** → error for one sample\n",
        "* **Cost** → average of all errors (overall model performance)\n",
        "\n",
        "**Examples:**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **For Regression:** Mean Squared Error (MSE)\n",
        "  ( L = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum(y_{true} - y_{pred})^2 )\n",
        "* **For Binary Classification:** Binary Cross-Entropy\n",
        "  ( L = -\\frac{1}{N}\\sum[y\\log(p) + (1-y)\\log(1-p)] )\n",
        "* **For Multi-Class Classification:** Categorical Cross-Entropy\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### **Question 5: What is the role of optimizers in neural networks? Compare Gradient Descent, Adam, and RMSprop.**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Role of Optimizers:**\n",
        "Optimizers adjust the **weights and biases** of the network to minimize the loss function during training. They control **how fast and effectively** the model learns.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "#### **Comparison Table:**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Optimizer                                  | Description                                                           | Strengths                             | Weaknesses                                      |\n",
        "| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n",
        "| **Gradient Descent (Batch / SGD)**         | Updates weights using gradient of cost function.                      | Simple, foundational                  | Slow convergence, may get stuck in local minima |\n",
        "| **RMSprop (Root Mean Square Propagation)** | Uses moving average of squared gradients for adaptive learning rates. | Works well for non-stationary data    | Sensitive to hyperparameters                    |\n",
        "| **Adam (Adaptive Moment Estimation)**      | Combines RMSprop + Momentum; adaptive learning rate + acceleration.   | Fast convergence, best for most tasks | May generalize worse in rare cases              |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "#### **Formulas:**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Gradient Descent:**\n",
        "  ( w = w - \\eta \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w} )\n",
        "\n",
        "* **RMSprop:**\n",
        "  ( v_t = \\beta v_{t-1} + (1 - \\beta)(\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w})^2 )\n",
        "  ( w = w - \\frac{\\eta}{\\sqrt{v_t + \\epsilon}}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w} )\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Adam:**\n",
        "  Combines momentum and RMSprop using moving averages of gradients (m) and squared gradients (v).\n",
        "\n",
        "---"
